  "metadata": {
    "title": "Cyclic covering perfect hash families: new covering arrays of strength five and six with exact certificates",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23848283",
    "publication_date": "2026-10-31",
    "description": "<p>A search over covering perfect hash families whose columns form one or a few cyclic orbits gives nine covering arrays smaller than the best sizes previously recorded, at strengths 5 and 6 and alphabets of size 3, 5, 7 and 9: CA(2663;5,121,3), CA(40613;5,781,5), CA(218479;5,2801,7), CA(10921;6,121,3), CA(249985;6,781,5), CA(49985;5,1562,5), CA(767625;5,1210,9), CA(268897;5,5602,7) and CA(885721;5,7381,9), and fusing one symbol of one of them gives a tenth, CA(249985;6,781,4). The nine lower the best known size at 11,453 points (t,k,v) of the table directly; fusing symbols and deriving lower strengths from them, by established constructions, lower the computed size at 16,891 further points. The largest gains are a factor of up to 3.5 at 1211 to 7381 columns for v=9, factors of 3.0 to 3.7 at 2000 to 5602 columns for v=7, and 1.7 to 4.25 at 721 to 781 columns for t=6, v=5. Because shifting the columns maps every row to itself up to an invertible matrix, it suffices to check the t-sets whose smallest column lies in the first orbit. Two checkers, written separately from the search and from each other and using different methods, verify this symmetry row by row and then check every representative t-set exactly, up to 1.23\u00d710^14 sets for one family; they agree on every family, and on the counts of uncovered sets for incomplete test families. The same kind of symmetry argument, run on a GPU, gives full checks of 31 binary cyclotomic arrays of strength 6 listed in the Colbourn tables. We also record limits of the method: with one column orbit and q a power of 2, families of this kind at t=5 leave certain subfield sets uncovered whatever rows are chosen. The families, programs, logs and hashes are public, so every result can be rebuilt and rechecked.</p><p>Version 3 adds CA(885721;5,7381,9), checked by both checkers, and CA(249985;6,781,4), obtained by fusing a symbol of CA(249985;6,781,5), and updates the counts of improved points. Version 2 added three arrays from families with several column orbits and a second, independent checker.</p><p>Repository: <a href=\"https://github.com/chrispbsmolen/ca-tools\">https://github.com/chrispbsmolen/ca-tools</a></p>",
